# JapanPad configuration.
#
# Copy to .env.local and edit. Nothing here is a secret in the usual sense —
# every NEXT_PUBLIC_ value is compiled into the browser bundle by definition,
# so treat them as published. The one variable that is NOT public is
# JAPANPAD_RPC_URL; see the note on it below.
#
# Defaults are deliberately safe: with this file empty, the app runs against
# testnet and the launch surface disables itself rather than encoding a
# transaction to an address nobody verified.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NETWORK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# mainnet | testnet | arc | arc-testnet | local. Both must be set and must
# agree — the app throws on boot if they disagree, because a server that thinks
# it is on testnet while serving a mainnet bundle would pop a wallet dialog on
# the wrong chain.
#
#   mainnet      Robinhood Chain 4663    real money   gas in ETH
#   testnet      Robinhood Chain 46630   play money   gas in ETH
#   arc          Arc 5042                real money   gas in USDC
#   arc-testnet  Arc 5042002             play money   gas in USDC
#   local        Anvil 31337             play money   gas in ETH
#
# Anything unrecognised resolves to testnet. A real-money network is opt-in and
# nothing else: it spends real funds and mints tokens that cannot be un-minted.
#
# Arc caveat, and it is the important one: Pons V2 is NOT deployed on Arc.
# Verified 2026-09-17 — eth_getCode at the factory answers 0x on both 5042 and
# 5042002. Pointed at Arc the app boots, reads the chain and prices in USDC, but
# the launch surface disables itself because there is no factory to call. Set
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PONS_V2_FACTORY once Pons ships there.
JAPANPAD_NETWORK=testnet
NEXT_PUBLIC_JAPANPAD_NETWORK=testnet


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RPC
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server-side only, and the only variable in this file that stays off the wire.
# Next.js does not expose a non-NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable to the browser, so an
# authenticated endpoint (Alchemy, a private node) belongs here. Every server
# read — the listings, the launch terms — goes through it.
#
# Leave unset to use the selected network's public endpoint.
# JAPANPAD_RPC_URL=https://your-private-endpoint
#
# On Arc this is not optional if you want trading to work. Pons has no quoter,
# so every price on the site comes from eth_simulateV1 with a state override —
# simulating the real buy against the real curve. Arc's public RPC does not
# implement that method; verified 2026-09-17, where Arc testnet answers
# literally "The method eth_simulateV1 does not exist / is not available" and
# Robinhood Chain answers the call fine.
#
# The consequence is specific: on Arc's public RPC, launching works and the
# trade panel cannot price anything. It shows an error rather than a made-up
# number, which is correct but not usable. Point JAPANPAD_RPC_URL at an Arc
# endpoint that supports eth_simulateV1 to fix it.

# The browser needs an endpoint too, for quotes and balances, and whatever is
# put here IS published. Never point this at an authenticated URL — it would
# ship the key to everyone who loads the page. Leave it unset unless you have a
# public endpoint you prefer to Robinhood's.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL=https://rpc.testnet.chain.robinhood.com


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PONS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pons's launch factory. On mainnet this defaults to the address verified on
# chain 4663 and only needs setting to override it. On testnet and local there
# is no default — Pons's testnet deployment is not something this repo has
# verified, and guessing would mean sending a launch into a contract that may
# not exist. Without it the launch page says so and refuses to build a
# transaction.
#
# Run `npm run verify:pons` after setting this. It reads the address on the
# live chain and fails loudly if there is no code there or if it does not
# answer Pons's ABI.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PONS_V2_FACTORY=0x...

# Per-chain overrides. The un-suffixed variable above applies to whichever
# network is active; these name a chain explicitly, which is what you want once
# more than one is reachable. A suffixed value wins for its own chain.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PONS_V2_FACTORY_MAINNET=0x...
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PONS_V2_FACTORY_TESTNET=0x...
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PONS_V2_FACTORY_ARC=0x...
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PONS_V2_FACTORY_ARC_TESTNET=0x...
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PONS_V2_FACTORY_LOCAL=0x...

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Arc: there IS a Pons-compatible factory, and it ships disabled on purpose.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pons V2 itself is not on Arc. But a fork of it is, and it answers the exact
# ABI this app already speaks. Measured on chain 5042 on 2026-09-17:
#
#   0xd6b86b9B1bB64b941b21AaA6a0e3A673e8405A3b   (published as "SolonPad")
#     eth_getCode              24,121 bytes
#     getLaunchConfig(0)       supply 1e27, curveFeeBps 100, phantomQuote
#                              4,000e18, graduationThreshold 10,000e18,
#                              tickSpacing 200, enabled true
#     previewLaunchEconomics   answers
#     TokenLaunched / curve / token ABIs   all read correctly
#
# So it works. Three reasons it is not a default anyway:
#
#   1. It first had code at block 21,134,269 — hours old at the time of writing.
#   2. It carries one token, "Solon Smoke" (SMOKE), launched by the factory's
#      own deployer, with 0.66 USDC in the curve.
#   3. Nobody has audited it, and Arc gas is real USDC. A default here spends
#      real money through an unreviewed contract the user never chose.
#
# Setting this is a decision to trust that contract. Run `npm run verify:pons`
# against it first, and re-read points 1-3.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PONS_V2_FACTORY_ARC=0xd6b86b9B1bB64b941b21AaA6a0e3A673e8405A3b

# Which of Pons's launch configs to use. 0 is the standard curve: 1B supply,
# 1% curve fee, 4.2 ETH graduation threshold, verified live on 2026-09-15.
# Pons's owner can add configs and disable old ones, so this is overridable
# rather than pinned in code.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PONS_LAUNCH_CONFIG_ID=0


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# INDEXING
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The L2 block to start scanning TokenLaunched events from — ideally the block
# Pons's factory was deployed at. Absent it, listings look back 400,000 blocks,
# which is fine for a fresh deployment and will quietly miss older launches on
# an established one.
#
# This must be a height that came from an RPC. Robinhood Chain is an Arbitrum
# Orbit rollup, so `block.number` read from inside a contract is the PARENT
# chain's height — about 36 million blocks off — and is not what eth_getLogs
# wants.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SCAN_FROM_BLOCK=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OPTIONAL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Canonical origin, for absolute URLs in metadata.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://japanpad.example

# Multicall3, for local chains only. Mainnet and testnet both have it at the
# canonical 0xcA11bde0… address, verified by calling getChainId() and comparing
# the answer, so this is ignored outside `local`. Without it, reads still work
# — they just cost one round trip per call instead of one per batch.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_MULTICALL3=0xcA11bde05977b3631167028862bE2a173976CA11
